+# GLM-5.3-Flash TP2 prefill 250 tok/s — phase 2: expert grouped-GEMM M-scaling (2026-10-10)
+
+Target: `BatchExpertGroupedKernel` (+ its histogram/prefix/scatter helpers and
+launcher), the MoE family that phase 1 measured at 242.9 ms per 512-token
+chunk (12.1% of slice kernel time; ~40% of the P5 *pair* profile).
+
+All numbers below: `gpu-test` build, model
+`/root/models/glm53-flash/GLM-5.3-Flash-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00005.gguf`,
+`--layers 4 --random-tokens <N> 1 --repeat 2` (BEST of the two passes;
+observed run-to-run noise ~±1%). Slice = layers 0–2 KDA (dense FFN) +
+layer 3 DSA/MoE (288 experts × 8 active, expert FFN 2048, Q4_K).
+
+## What actually bounded the kernel
+
+The phase-1 thesis ("weights re-read 3.6× per chunk, so raise M") was only a
+third of the story. Measured sequence:
+
+1. **M=16 alone (V2) changed nothing** (345 → 345 tok/s; kernel time
+   251 ms/chunk). The weight re-reads disappeared, but each block re-stages
+   the pair tile's M activation rows from global x once per
+   `m / (warps · rows)` row groups — at M=16/W=8 that x traffic
+   (~19 GB/chunk) grew as fast as the weight traffic (~10.6 GB) shrank.
+2. **Widening the block (W=16) was worth +10.7%**: same LDS staging
+   amortized over twice the rows halves the x traffic.
+3. **A per-warp row loop (R=2) on top** reached the current plateau:
+   one staging pass feeds W·R = 32 rows.
+4. **Instruction-diet changes (float2 LDS reads, int8→float hoist,
+   stride-33→34 padding) were neutral** at the plateau — the kernel is
+   memory-traffic/latency-bound at ~34.0 KiB LDS (1 block per 64 KiB LDS
+   unit, probed via `hipOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor`:
+   16.9 KiB→3 blocks/SM, 33.0 KiB→1, >64 KiB impossible on gfx1151).
+
+Final per-chunk expert family: **242.9 → 81.3 ms (2.99×)**, 12.1% → 6.2%
+of slice kernel time; remaining ~81 ms ≈ 4.1 GB weights-once + ~5.9 GB
+x-staging at ~125 GB/s effective — further gains need either smaller x
+staging (bigger W·R runs into register/LDS walls: W8R8 and W32R2 both
+lose) or L2-locality work, not more M.
+
+## Variant sweep (512-token bench, tok/s)
+
+Baseline V0 = phase-1 tree (`kTileTokens=4, kTileK=1024, kTileWarps=8`,
+one row per warp).
+
+| Variant (M, K, warps, rows/warp) | LDS staging | tok/s | vs V0 | gates |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| V0 baseline | 16.5 KiB | 342.7 / 347.1 | — | phase-1 set all PASS |
+| V2: 16, 512, 8, 1 | 33.0 KiB | 344.1 / 345.2 | +0% | not gated (rejected) |
+| V2 + diet, 16, 512, 16, 1 | 34.0 KiB | 381.3 / 381.5 | +10.6% | — |
+| 16, 512, 16, 2 | 34.0 KiB | 386.2 | +11.9% | — |
+| 16, 512, 16, 4 | 34.0 KiB | 381.8 / 385.2 / 386.2 | +11.5% | — |
+| 16, 512, 32, 4 | 34.0 KiB | 383.9 / 384.5 / 387.4 | +11.6% | — |
+| **+ float4 staging, 16, 512, 16, 2 (WINNER)** | 34.0 KiB | **390.1 / 390.2 / 390.6** | **+13.0%** | full set PASS (below) |
+| 16, 512, 32, 2 | 34.0 KiB | 385.4 / 385.5 | +11.7% | not gated (rejected) |
+| 16, 512, 8, 8 | 34.0 KiB | 366.0 / 366.3 | +6.1% | not gated (rejected) |
+| 8, 512, 16, 2 (M-sweep completion) | 17.0 KiB | 390.2 / 389.3 | +13.0% (tie) | not gated (rejected, see below) |
+| 8, 512, 16, 4 | 17.0 KiB | 387.9 / 387.8 | +12.3% | not gated (rejected) |
+| 8, 512, 8, 4 (3 blocks/SM) | 17.0 KiB | 385.1 / 388.0 / 388.7 | +12.2% | not gated (rejected) |
+
+M-sweep verdict ({8, 16, 32} commanded): M=32 is infeasible as a like-for-like
+shape (K=1024 staging would need 69.6 KiB > the 64 KiB gfx1151 LDS cap;
+K=256 idles half the sub16 warp), M=8 and M=16 measured. The M=8 re-measure
+ran same-session against fresh winner runs (390.0 / 390.0 / 390.8): the
+closest M=8 shape (W16R2, same 32 rows per staging pass) ties inside the
+±1% noise band (389.3–390.2) with no win beyond it, and every other M=8
+shape is 2–5 tok/s below. M=8 is rejected on traffic grounds at equal
+speed: a ~14-pair bucket needs ⌈14/8⌉ = 2 tiles per expert, doubling the
+per-chunk expert weight stream (~4.1 → ~8.2 GB) for zero measured gain —
+and the disadvantage grows with bucket occupancy (hot experts, longer
+chunks). The occupancy counter-argument was tested directly: M=8/W8R4
+keeps the winner's 32 rows per staging pass at 17.0 KiB LDS, which the
+occupancy probe places at 3 blocks/SM (vs the winner's 1) — it measured
+385.1–388.7, i.e. the extra resident blocks do *not* pay, confirming the
+kernel is bound by the x/weight byte stream, not by wave count. (An
+independent replication of the M=8 shapes in the verification round,
+separate build tree, landed in the same 380–388 band.) M=16 stays landed.
+Measurement hygiene note: three early single runs were mislabeled by a sed
+that did not match (`kExpertRows` stayed 4 while labeled R1/R2); every
+shortlisted shape was re-measured cleanly on the final code before choosing
+— the winner's margin over W32R2/W16R4 (≈5 tok/s ≈ +1.3%) exceeds the
+±1% noise band across 3 clean runs each.
+
+## Winner (landed)
+
+`kExpertTokens=16, kExpertK=512, kExpertWarps=16, kExpertRows=2`
+(32 output rows per 34.0 KiB staging pass, 512 threads, one block per
+64 KiB LDS unit), plus four arithmetic-order-neutral restructures:
+
+- `xrow[]` LDS table: pair→x-row offsets (incl. the `/slots` division)
+  derived once per pair tile instead of per staged element;
+- stride-34 padded staging (8-byte aligned blocks, bank rotation
+  `(2·block + j) mod 32`) with **float2** consumer reads;
+- **float4** global staging reads with a scalar fallback for odd k/x_stride
+  (`vec_ok` guard keeps arbitrary geometry launch-safe);
+- int8→float weight conversion hoisted out of the per-token loop;
+- per-warp row loop R=2 with `sums[kExpertRows][kExpertTokens]`
+  accumulators (the r-loop adds rows, never changes any per-element K
+  accumulation order).
+
+### Numerics decision (recorded per phase contract)
+
+- **K-regroup**: `kExpertK` 1024→512 changes the per-lane partial grouping
+  (sub16: one sub-block per lane per 512-wide tile vs two per 1024-wide
+  tile). Chunk-vs-tokenwise byte equality is by construction — the
+  tokenwise path (`FfnBlock` → `FfnBlockBatch(n=1)`) launches the *same*
+  kernel — and the CPU oracle passed at **7.576e-07** worst logits rel_rms
+  (cap 2e-4; phase-1 value 7.6e-7), so the regrouping is well inside the
+  projection RMS contract. exec-513/exec-2052 byte equality passed.
+- The load/address restructures (float2/float4, stride 34, xrow, row loop)
+  load identical values in identical per-element add order: bit-exact by
+  construction and confirmed by `max|diff| = 0` in the exec gates.
+- Expert-routing determinism untouched: histogram/prefix/scatter and the
+  bucket order are unchanged; per-pair outputs remain single-writer.
+
+## Before/after (same binaries, back-to-back)
+
+| Measurement | V0 | winner | Δ |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Slice 512-token bench (this phase's commanded run) | 345 tok/s | 390.3 tok/s | **+13.0%** |
+| Slice 4096-token bench (phase-1 reference point) | 254.0 tok/s | 277.2 tok/s | **+9.1%** |
+| TP2 rank-0 slice 512-token bench (`--tp-rank 0`) | 681.9 tok/s | 764.2 tok/s | **+12.1%** |
+| Expert family per 512-chunk (rocprofv3, prof.py) | 242.9 ms | 81.3 ms | **2.99×** |
+| Decode guard: tokenwise avg (`executor_test --timing`, tokens=1) | 17.35 / 17.44 ms/tok | 16.80 / 17.04 ms/tok | ~3% faster |
+
+The decode path shares these kernels (`pcount=1` tiles); it got slightly
+*faster* because the per-block x staging redundancy also dropped
+(m/8 → m/32 row groups). No decode-latency regression.
+
