+# GLM-5.3-Flash TP2 prefill 250 tok/s — phase 7: residual iteration + final verify (2026-10-10)
+
+## Goal status: NOT MET — closed honestly with a quantitative roofline
+
+**Final serving-level prefill: 84.4 tok/s (84.17–84.74 across 3 calibrated
+cold-prefix runs, ~3.4K-token streamed prompts, context 32768, chunk 1024,
+f32 wire, census off) against the ≥ 250 tok/s objective — a 3.0× gap.**
+The goal's iteration mandate has hit the machine: Section "Roofline" shows
+that with the current correctness contracts (byte-identical chunked-vs-
+tokenwise paths, CPU-oracle RMS caps, exact-order KDA scan), 250 tok/s
+would require the weight-moving kernels to stream faster than the measured
+peak DRAM read bandwidth of this GPU. The best-achieved numbers, the
+per-family evidence and the escape routes are all recorded below. The
+target is not fudged; the campaign closes at 84.4 tok/s serving.
+
+Campaign trajectory at serving level: 35.1 (P5 baseline) → 75.0 (phase 6)
+→ **84.4 tok/s (this phase)**.
+
+## What changed this phase (kernel-by-kernel)
+
+One optimization iteration landed, one bounded variant measured and
+rejected. Both stay inside the correctness contracts: the two KDA kernels
+are the only functional source changes.
+
+1. **`BatchKdaRecurrenceKernel` / `KdaRecurrenceKernel` →
+   `*RegKernel<128>` fast path** (kernels_batch.hip.cpp, kernels.hip.cpp;
+   both twins rewritten identically). The KDA delta-rule scan is one block
+   per head, 128 threads, each thread owning one value column of the
+   [128×128] head state and walking tokens in the reference's exact serial
+   order. The generic kernel round-tripped every state cell through
+   **global memory twice per token** and recomputed all 128 `expf` decays
+   per thread per token (128× threads redundant). The fast path keeps the
+   thread's state column in registers across the whole token loop and
+   stages the three block-uniform per-token rows (decay, k, q) once per
+   block in LDS. The statements, their order and the serial per-thread
+   accumulation over d are unchanged, so:
+   - chunked vs tokenwise byte identity holds by construction (same
+     kernel per token arithmetic, no slot dependence), and
+   - the rewrite is **bit-identical to the pre-change binary**: the
+     5-token executor oracle reports the same rel_rms 7.484e-07 as phases
+     3–6, and the final-tree pair probe reproduces the pre-change
+     binary's per-token hash lines byte-for-byte (9/9 decode lines,
+     `t=`, `top`, `h=`, `rms` identical; only wall `ms=` differs).
+   - `__launch_bounds__(kDim, 1)` buys the compiler the register budget
+     (192 VGPRs, 308 B scratch → slice bench +2.5%); applied to both twins.
+   - Measured: pair rank0 KDA-recurrence kernel time 3439.7 → 245.2 ms
+     per 2048 tokens (**14.0×**, 13.4% → 1.1% of kernel time); slice
+     bench 713.5 → 835.7 tok/s (+17%); pair probe 78.3 → 89.2 tok/s
+     (+14%); tokenwise decode twin 22.3 → 5.7 ms (3.9×); decode guard
+     16.74 ms/token (16.6–16.9 band, unchanged within noise).
+2. **Expert tile retune at the serving chunk width (rejected)**. Phase 2
+   chose `kExpertTokens=16` at 512-wide chunks (14.2 pairs/expert);
+   serving now runs 1024-wide chunks (28.4 pairs/expert, 2 tiles per
+   expert either way at T=16). Re-benched T=8 vs T=16 at both widths:
+   T=8 gives 814.7 vs 818.3 tok/s at 1024-wide and 829.2 vs 831.3 at
+   512-wide (−0.25…−0.4%, outside noise). T=16 stays; T=32 remains
+   LDS-infeasible (68 KiB > 64 KiB). Recorded as the confirmation that
+   the phase-2 corner holds at the new chunk size.
+
+## Before/after (same-session measurements)
+
