+# GLM-5.3-Flash TP2 prefill 250 tok/s — phase 3 (vllm goal): Q8_0
+# QuantTiled weight-stationary GEMM (2026-10-11)
+
+Target: `BatchQuantTiledKernel` (Q8_0/sub16 dense projections), the family
+the phase brief puts at 19% of pair kernel time / 122 GB/s ("51% of the read
+ceiling") with a 4300 → ≤1500 ms per 2048-token mandate and a re-read factor
+to push from 64× toward 1-2×. All prototype numbers:
+`tools/bench/quant_ws_bench.hip` (standalone HIP via `tools/bench/build.sh`),
+real TP1 shapes dumped from the artifact (kda.q 8192×4096, kda.out
+4096×8192, dense.gate 12288×4096, dense.down 4096×12288, dsa.q_b
+16384×1536, dsa.out 4096×16384, plus a Q4_K 12288×4096 control for the
+sub16 branch), synthetic blocks with realistic layout/scales, random
+activations, 5-iteration means after 2 warmups. Integrated numbers:
+`gpu-test` build of the phase-2 tree (`a706c4b`), model
+`/root/models/glm53-flash/GLM-5.3-Flash-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00005.gguf`.
+
+**Outcome: no kernel change lands.** The swept restructures — the exact
+treatment the brief sketches (blocks owning wider token tiles, table streamed
+once per tile, M-tiled register accumulators) — all measure 1.2-2× slower
+than the landed kernel, and the mandate arithmetic itself is at the hardware
+ceiling: the family's 4300 ms/2048 tokens **is** its DRAM stream at ~99% of
+the measured 240.24 GB/s peak once the x-restaging term is counted, and the
+≤1500 ms target is unreachable even at zero weight re-read (roofline below).
+The winner of the sweep is the landed `BatchQuantTiledKernel`
+(kQuantTokens=16 / kQuantK=512 / kQuantWarps=32 / kQuantRows=2) — dispatch
+unchanged, single canonical path for every n including the n=1 vocabulary
+head (0.67 GB), which the small-n table shows is also the fastest shape at
+n=1 (0.127 ms vs 0.128-0.49 for every alternative).
+
+## The traffic model (validated three ways)
+
+Per chunk of n tokens, per projection of m rows over k columns
+(Q8_0 = 1.0625 B/elt; rpb = warps·rows = 64 rows sharing one staged tile):
+
+- weights: `m·k·1.0625 · n/T` — re-read once per T-wide token tile;
+- x-restaging: `(m/rpb) · n · k · 4` — every block re-fetches its tile's
+  activations from global through LDS;
+- y writes: `n·m·4` (small).
+
+At the landed T16/rpb64 the x term is 0.94× the weight term — the same
+lesson as the expert family (notes/experts-ws-250.md): **the second stream
+is as big as the first**. Validation:
+
+| Context | analytic traffic | measured time | effective BW |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| standalone kda.q n=1024 (bench9) | 4.28 GB | 18.87 ms | 227 GB/s |
+| standalone dense.gate n=1024 | 6.65 GB | 28.54 ms | 233 GB/s |
+| standalone dsa.q_b n=1024 | 3.32 GB | 15.66 ms | 212 GB/s |
+| slice in-situ, 512-chunk (fresh profile, 16 chunks) | 58.4 GB | 294.5 ms | 198 GB/s |
+| pair mandate basis, 1024-chunk/rank | 509 GB | 2150 ms (4300/2048) | 236 GB/s |
+
+(The brief's "122 GB/s = 51%" divided only the 262 GB weight term by the
+family time; adding the 246 GB x-restaging term puts the pair family at the
+ceiling. The model's pair prediction — 4240 ms/2048 at 240.24 GB/s — matches
+the mandated 4300 within 1.4%.)
+
+**Mandate roofline (per rank, per 2048 tokens = 2×1024-chunks, at the
+240.24 GB/s ceiling):**
+
+| tile | weights | x-restaging | total | floor @240 GB/s |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| landed T16/rpb64 | 524 GB | 492 GB | 1017 GB | **4240 ms** (measured 4300) |
+| best-legal T32/rpb64 | 262 GB | 492 GB | 757 GB | 3150 ms |
+| register-illegal T64/rpb64 | 131 GB | 492 GB | 625 GB | 2600 ms |
+| weights-once fantasy (T=1024) | 8.2 GB | 492 GB | 503 GB | **2090 ms** |
+
+The ≤1500 ms mandate would need ≥340 GB/s on today's traffic or a total
+stream ≤ 360 GB — **unreachable even at zero weight re-read** with the
+current x-audience (rpb 64). Cutting family time further is an
+executor-level x-audience problem (wider row blocks per staged tile, shared
+staging across gate/up, or K-split staging), not a tile-geometry problem —
+the same conclusion phase 2 reached for the expert family's x-restaging
+term.
+
+## Variant sweep (the treatment the brief sketches, measured)
+
+Families: **W** = no LDS, x streamed from global per warp with T-wide
+register accumulators; **L** = LDS group re-staging (V0's padded 16-token
+tile re-staged T/16 times per K-tile, sums resident across the K stream);
+**Z** = unpadded XOR-swizzled LDS ([T][512] fits 64 KiB exactly at T=32:
+element e stored at `(e&~31) | ((e&31) ^ (e>>5))`, conflict-free for the
+lane-L 16-run reads). All keep V0's per-(row, token) arithmetic — lane run
+assignment, ascending visit order, statement sequence, single butterfly —
+so all are bit-identical to V0 by construction (verified, incl. the
+executor twin pattern A(n=N) == V0(n=1 × N) at n=513/1024 on every shape).
+
+Resources (hipClang -Rpass) and ms/call (BEST of 5, `/tmp/opencode/
+quantws/bench9.log` — the corrected run, see the harness note below; kda.q
+and dense.gate at n=1024, the serving chunk width):
+
+| Variant (T, path) | VGPR / waves | LDS | scratch B/lane | kda.q | dense.gate | vs V0 | re-read @1024 |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| **V0 16/512/32/2 (landed)** | 95 / 16 | 33.8 KiB | 0 | **18.87** | **28.54** | 1.00× | 64× |
+| Z32R2 (swizzle, 64 KiB) | 183 / 8 | 64.0 KiB | 0 | 24.05 | 36.39 | 1.27× | 32× |
+| Z16R2 (swizzle control) | 110 / 12 | 32.0 KiB | 0 | 24.56 | 36.91 | 1.30× | 64× |
+| L32G16 (group re-stage) | 130 / 10 | 33.8 KiB | 0 | 24.60 | 37.24 | 1.30× | 32× |
+| L64G16R1 (group re-stage) | 136 / 10 | 33.8 KiB | 0 | 27.10 | 40.64 | 1.44× | 16× |
+| W32R2 (global-x) | 139 / 10 | 0 | 0 | 31.10 | 48.85 | 1.65× | 32× |
+| W64R1s (global-x, scalar) | 171 / 8 | 0 | 0 | 35.13 | 52.66 | 1.86× | 16× |
+| W64R1 (global-x, float4) | 171 / 8 | 0 | 0 | 36.74 | 55.23 | 1.95× | 16× |
+| Z32R1 (swizzle, 64 KiB) | 118 / 12 | 64.0 KiB | 0 | 25.75 | 38.67 | 1.36× | 32× |
+
+The ordering holds at every width (96/513/1024/2048), on all seven shapes
+including the Q4_K sub16 control (dsa.out best-alt 1.21×, gate.q4k 1.21×)
+and at every small n (V0 fastest at n ∈ {1, 8, 16, 32, 64}; Z16R2 ties at
+n=1 within 1%).
+
+Rejected before the final run (resources/measured in the earlier logs,
+`/tmp/opencode/quantws/bench{1,4,6}.log` — with the caveat that those runs
+predate the harness fix below, so their L/W numbers are upper bounds):
+
+- **Scratch-demoted accumulators** (sums > ~64 floats/lane → ScratchSize
+  224-1040 B/lane): W64R2, W64R2x16, W32R4, W128R1 (+455 B spills),
+  W128R1x8/x16, W128R1s, W256R1, L64G16R2, L128G16(x16), L128G8, L256G16 —
+  1.7-5× slower wherever they run (e.g. W64R2 98.3 ms and W256R1 92.9 at
+  kda.q/1024 in bench1, where their grids were correct), **and the
+  global-x scratch variants deterministically fault (illegal address)
+  under back-to-back concurrent launches** on this stack (rocm 7.2.3).
+  Removed from the sweep; T=128 with global x spills even at 256 VGPRs.
+- vLLM's W8A16 linears (the brief's analog) win via fp16-activation GEMM:
+  the int8→fp16 weight side is exact, but fp16 x is not — rejected on the
+  chunked-vs-tokenwise byte-identity contract (no relaxation available for
+  this family).
+
+### Harness correction (retracted claims)
+
+bench1-bench8 contain two artifacts of a bench bug, fixed before the final
+run: the variant-token-width switch fell through to 16 for the L/Z family,
+so those launches overshot grid.y by T/16×. `tokens = min(T, n - token_base)`
+then wrapped unsigned for the overshoot blocks, which computed dead work
+into unused buffer tails (in-bounds at most shapes: ~2× inflated L/Z times)
+and overran the y buffer exactly at the shapes that "faulted" (m=12288 ×
+n≥1536) — the apparent "T=32 faults past 8k blocks" stack defect was **this
+harness bug, not a silicon/ROCm defect, and is retracted**. The scratch
+fault above is unaffected (those variants had correct grids throughout).
+bench9 is the authoritative run: correct grids, rc=0, 140/140
+identity checks, every width including 2048.
+
+### Why every restructure loses (measured, not modeled)
+
+1. **The landed kernel is already at the DRAM roofline.** 212-241 GB/s
+   standalone across six shapes (88-100% of the 240.24 GB/s peak), 198 GB/s
+   in-situ on the slice. A traffic cut only converts to time if the new
+   kernel sustains `old_BW × traffic_ratio` — T32's 1.35× cut would need
+   ≥310 GB/s. Over the hardware. The measured T=32 shapes (Z32R2, L32G16,
+   1.27-1.30× slower) sit exactly where that arithmetic predicts.
+2. **x must come from LDS.** The W family (global x, no barriers, 4-8× less
+   DRAM) runs 1.65-1.95× slower: 200-cycle global latency against ~16
+   FMA-cycles of dependent work per token, with no register budget to keep
+   ≥10 tokens in flight. LDS broadcast staging is what buys V0 its overlap.
+3. **T > 16 with LDS costs either padding or conflicts.** Padded [32][512]
+   is 66 KiB (cap 64); the XOR swizzle that fits exactly costs 30% at T=16
+   (Z16R2 control — the pattern defeats b128 LDS reads, 16 scalar reads per
+   run) and 27% at T=32 on top of the wider-tile losses. Group re-staging
+   (L) pays barriers + weight L2 re-reads per group: 1.30-1.44×.
+4. **Registers cap sums at ~64 floats/lane.** Beyond that the compiler
+   demotes to private scratch (measured 1.7-5× slower + the concurrency
+   faults above). With W ≤ 32 warps (1024-thread cap) and rpb = W·R, the
+   x-term floor 4/rpb and the weight floor 1.0625/T minimize at T ≈ 23 —
+   the best legal point is T32/rpb64 at a 1.35× traffic cut that cannot
+   convert (point 1).
+
+The re-read factor therefore stays at chunk/16 (64× per 1024-token serving
+chunk, 32× per 512-token slice chunk): every measured path below it is
+slower, and the roofline shows the weight term is not the binding stream —
+x-restaging is (equal magnitude, and it is what makes even weights-once
+floor at 2090 ms/2048).
+
